.TH GARDE 1 "2026-07-30" "bashified" "Zach's utilities"
.SH NAME
garde \- guard the estate's data: nightly backups and their proof
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B garde
[\fIsubcommand\fR] [\fIflags\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A plain shell utility. It takes arguments, keeps a stated promise, and
exits with a code that says exactly what happened.
.PP
Run
.B garde list
to see the subcommands that are actually backed by tooling.
A subcommand the contract names but for which no tooling exists exits 4
(GAP) and says so, rather than exiting 0 having done nothing.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Implemented for
.BR "media list" ", " "media audit" ", " "media run" ", " git ", " rules ", " backup ", " install ", and " uninstall
(via
.BR jq (1);
see MEDIA, GIT and MANIFEST EDITING below for each shape).
Every other subcommand still refuses it with a usage error rather than a
silent no\-op. See
.BR fauche (1)
for the sibling implementation.
.TP
.B \-\-quiet, \-q
Results only, no commentary.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Usage summary.
.SH THE COST BOUNDARY
This utility does not spend money and therefore has
.B no --summon flag.
Any utility in this family that CAN spend declares
.B \-\-summon
and refuses to spend without it. There is deliberately no short form:
.B \-s
collides with existing tools and
.B \-S
differs from it by a
single shift key, which is an unacceptable property for the one flag
that costs real money. Spelling the word out is the deliberateness.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The promise was kept.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error; the caller is wrong.
.TP
.B 3
Needs a summon and did not get one. A finding, not an error.
.TP
.B 4
GAP: the tooling to keep this promise does not exist yet.
.TP
.B 5
BROKEN: it ran and produced a wrong or partial answer.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND: it cannot read its domain, so it will not report on it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I CONTRACT.md
The promise this utility must keep.
.TP
.I GAPS.md
What the contract names and the tooling cannot yet do.
.SH HISTORY
Rebuilt 2026-07-30 from the tree named
.BR gardien .
This branch is a total purge: it keeps the tool and nothing else.
What was removed is not lost \- it is on the default branch
of this same repository. That is why this is a branch and not a new
repository, and extracting it into one would destroy the archive that
justifies the purge.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dose (1)
.SH MANIFEST EDITING
.TP
.B garde add <pattern> --set <name>
Add an include glob pattern to an existing set's
.B include
array, deduplicated. Refuses to create a new set
.RI ( gardien #32
scopes this to editing an existing set's rules, not authoring new sets).
Not yet enforced by
.BR "garde media run" ;
.B garde rules
labels every include line with this gap.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { ok ", " set ", " pattern ", " added }
object instead of the confirmation line (
.B added
is
.B false
when the pattern was already present \(em the write is idempotent either
way).
.TP
.B "garde exclude <pattern> --set <name>|--global"
Add an exclude glob pattern, either to one set's existing
.B exclude
array (already read by
.B media run
at copy time) or, with
.BR --global ,
to a manifest-wide
.B global_exclude
array that is meant to override every set-level include once
.B include
is wired into the copy engine. Exactly one of
.B --set
or
.B --global
is required.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { ok ", " set " or " global ", " pattern ", " added }
object instead of the confirmation line, the same
.B added
idiom as
.BR "garde add" .
.TP
.B "garde rules [set]"
Print the effective rule set: global excludes first, then each set's
path, exclude and include patterns. Read-only.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { global_exclude ", " sets }
object instead of the text report, where each entry of
.B sets
is
.RB { name ", " path ", " exclude ", " include }.
.PP
All three validate the generated manifest as parseable JSON in a temp
file
.I before
it ever replaces the live one, and take a timestamped
.I .bak
copy on every write. This is the only sanctioned way to edit
.BR garde.json ;
a hand edit has none of these guarantees.
.SH GIT
.TP
.B garde git <path>
For a git repository, "backed up" means every commit is on a remote and
nothing is uncommitted, untracked (and not ignored), or stashed \(em a
predicate over repository state, not a file transfer
.RI ( gardien #33).
Checks every local branch against its own
.I origin/<branch>
ref, not just the one checked out, so an unpushed feature branch is
caught even from a clean, detached
.BR HEAD .
Exits 0 only if fully pushed and clean; exits 5 and lists every issue
found otherwise; exits 6 (BLIND) if the path is not a readable git
repository at all. Never spends money \(em this is a local predicate
git already answers.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { repo ", " backed_up ", " reasons }
object instead of the text report.
.SH BACKUP
.TP
.B garde backup [\-\- gardien.py-flags...]
Wraps the nightly snapshot rotator,
.B gardien.py
(kept on the
.B main
branch;
.RI ( gardien #26).
Execs it at
.BR $GARDIEN_REPO/gardien.py
with
.BR "\-\-config $GARDIEN_CONFIG" ,
relaying its exit code: 0 passes through as 0, any other exit is reported
BROKEN (5). Exits 4 (GAP) if
.B gardien.py
is not found at
.BR $GARDIEN_REPO .
gardien.py\-specific flags
.RI ( "\-\-only" ", " "\-\-check\-stale" ", " ...)
are not in this verb's own flag set and must follow
.BR \-\- .
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { ok ", " exit_code }
object instead of relying on gardien.py's own stdout/stderr for the verdict
(GAP stays plain text either way, the same as every other subcommand's
unreadable\-domain case).
.SH MEDIA
.TP
.B garde media list
What each set is, where it is held, and which sets sit below their
declared \fBmin_copies\fR floor. Exit 6 (BLIND) if no destination is
reachable \(em an unreadable domain is never reported as an empty one.
.B ok
means the set was proven byte\-for\-byte and nothing in it has changed
since;
.B STALE
means its file count still satisfies \fBmin_copies\fR but a local file
has changed or been created since that proof, so the count is no longer
evidence of anything current
.RI ( gardien #21).
A STALE set is picked back up by
\fBgarde media run --all-pending\fR, the same as a short one.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { set ,
.BR local ,
.BR remote ,
.BR held ,
.BR min_copies ,
.BR status }
object per set instead of the table (
.B remote
is
.B null
when no destination was reachable for that set).
.TP
.B garde media run [set...] [--all-pending]
Copy each set to every destination it declares, then prove it landed by
hashing both sides locally and comparing digests. Exits 0 only if every
set was copied AND proven.
\fB\-\-json\fR silences the progress commentary and emits one
.RB { set ", " dest ", " "status: " "\(dqok\(dq" | "\(dqunreachable\(dq" | "\(dqcopy_failed\(dq" | "\(dqverify_failed\(dq" }
object per (set, destination) pair attempted, then a final
.RB { ok ", " ran ", " failed }
summary object (
.B ran
is the count of sets named, not the count of pairs). BROKEN/STALE
diagnostics from a failed copy or verify still print to stderr as plain
text either way.
.TP
.B garde media audit
Exit 5 if any set is below its floor, or if any set's manifest name
differs from its path's basename \(em a copy lands under the basename, so
such a set reads as if it went somewhere it did not.
\fB\-\-json\fR emits one
.RB { type: " below_floor" | "misnamed" ,
.B ...}
object per finding, then a final
.RB { ok ", " below_floor ", " misnamed }
summary object.
.TP
.B garde media triage <set> --summon
Diagnose a recorded hash mismatch. Needs a summon; the cost is read from
a recorded measurement, and the first run records it.
.SH "SHOULD DO VS WILL NOT"
Exit 4 (GAP) means in scope but unbuilt: summon an agent or do it by
hand, then mechanize it. Exit 7 (REFUSED) means out of scope by design
and never to be built; no \fB--summon\fR is offered. \fBgarde media
restore\fR and \fBgarde media sync\fR are refusals, not gaps.
